            "result_text": "Done! The function uses `asyncio.Semaphore` to limit concurrency. Here's how it works:\n\n- **Semaphore**: Controls how many tasks run at once—each task acquires the semaphore before running\n- **Keyboard interrupt handling**: When interrupted, it cancels remaining tasks and waits for them all to finish with `return_exceptions=True`, ensuring any `finally` blocks or cleanup code runs before re-raising the interrupt\n\nYou can import and use it like:\n\n```python\nfrom run import run_tasks\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def my_task():\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(1)\n        print(\"Task done\")\n    finally:\n        print(\"Cleanup\")\n\nasyncio.run(run_tasks([my_task, my_task, my_task], max_concurrent=2))\n```"
